<main id="main-content" class="experience-page"><section class="page-hero"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT TO EXPECT</p><h1>What happens at a<br><em>Toastmasters meeting?</em></h1><p class="lead">If you have never been to Toastmasters, the format can seem unfamiliar. Here is a simple guide to what you may see during an Avon meeting.</p></div></section><section class="section"><div class="wrap"><div class="timeline" aria-label="Five stages of a typical Avon Toastmasters meeting"><article class="reveal"><div class="timeline-no">01</div><div><p class="eyebrow blue">WELCOME</p><h2>Meet the people</h2><p>The meeting begins with a welcome and introductions. A member can help you understand the programme and where to sit. As a guest, you can simply observe.</p></div></article><article class="reveal"><div class="timeline-no">02</div><div><p class="eyebrow blue">PREPARED SPEECHES</p><h2>Ideas brought to life</h2><p>Members deliver speeches they have planned and prepared. You will see different styles, topics and approaches — and how practice helps speakers improve.</p></div></article><article class="reveal"><div class="timeline-no">03</div><div><p class="eyebrow blue">TABLE TOPICS®</p><h2>Think on your feet</h2><p>Participants are invited to speak briefly on an unexpected question or topic. You are not required to take part as a guest; watching is absolutely fine.</p></div></article><article class="reveal"><div class="timeline-no">04</div><div><p class="eyebrow blue">EVALUATIONS</p><h2>Feedback that helps</h2><p>Speakers receive feedback that highlights what worked and offers ideas for their next step. Evaluation is a skill in itself: specific, thoughtful and supportive.</p></div></article><article class="reveal"><div class="timeline-no">05</div><div><p class="eyebrow blue">WRAP-UP</p><h2>Reflect and connect</h2><p>The meeting closes with a review and an opportunity to talk. Ask questions, meet members and find out how the learning programme works.</p></div></article></div></div></section><section class="section tint"><div class="wrap two-col"><div><p class="eyebrow blue">A NOTE FOR FIRST-TIME GUESTS</p><h2>You don't need<br><em>to prepare a speech.</em></h2></div><div class="copy"><p class="large">You can come along, take a seat and enjoy the meeting. There is no expectation that you will speak or join on the spot.</p><p>If you would like to participate in a small way, let a member know. We can explain the options and help you feel at ease.</p><a class="arrow-link" href="/visit.html">Plan your first visit →</a></div></div></section><section class="section tint"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow blue">TOASTMASTERS MEETING ROLES</p><h2>Discover the roles<br><em>behind every meeting.</em></h2></div><p>Members share responsibility for creating a well-run, interesting meeting. Roles rotate, so people can try new challenges as their experience grows. Guests are welcome to watch how each role works.</p></div><div class="role-grid" aria-label="Common Toastmasters meeting roles"><article class="role-card reveal"><span>01</span><h3>Toastmaster of the Day</h3><p>Plans, co-ordinates and hosts the meeting. The Toastmaster introduces participants, keeps the programme moving and makes sure transitions between speakers and activities feel smooth. This role is generally taken by members who are familiar with the club and its meeting format.</p></article><article class="role-card reveal"><span>02</span><h3>Timer or Timekeeper</h3><p>Helps speakers practise expressing ideas within an agreed time. The Timer tracks each section and signals when a speaker is approaching or has reached the time limit. The familiar green, yellow and red signals help everyone keep to time.</p></article><article class="role-card reveal"><span>03</span><h3>Introducer</h3><p>Introductions set the scene for a speaker and their presentation. The Introducer shares relevant information about the speaker and the speech or project, helping the audience understand what to listen for.</p></article><article class="role-card reveal"><span>04</span><h3>Speaker</h3><p>Prepared speeches are a central part of a Toastmasters meeting. Members plan and deliver presentations based on objectives in the Toastmasters Pathways learning experience. Topics can be wide-ranging while the speaker works on a particular communication skill.</p></article><article class="role-card reveal"><span>05</span><h3>Speech Evaluator</h3><p>Feedback is a cornerstone of the programme. The evaluator listens carefully and offers a thoughtful, respectful assessment, recognising what worked and suggesting practical ways to improve. A helpful structure is commendations, recommendations and further commendations.</p></article><article class="role-card reveal"><span>06</span><h3>Grammarian</h3><p>Encourages members to use language thoughtfully and expand their vocabulary. The Grammarian introduces a Word of the Day, listens for effective language and notes examples of interesting, clear or especially useful expression.</p></article><article class="role-card reveal"><span>07</span><h3>Table Topics Master</h3><p>Leads the impromptu speaking session by preparing questions or prompts, often connected to the meeting theme. Table Topics gives members a chance to practise thinking clearly and speaking without a prepared script.</p></article><article class="role-card reveal"><span>08</span><h3>Table Topics Speaker</h3><p>Responds to an unexpected question with a short, spontaneous answer. A response might be serious, informative, opinion-based or humorous. Guests may be invited to introduce themselves, but there is never pressure to participate.</p></article><article class="role-card reveal"><span>09</span><h3>General Evaluator</h3><p>Reviews the meeting as a whole and evaluates the supporting meeting roles, rather than the prepared speeches. The General Evaluator observes how the meeting flows and offers constructive feedback to help the club improve.</p></article></div><p class="muted">Meeting roles may vary from one meeting to another. New members are supported as they learn what each role involves.</p></div></section></main>